    <h2>The three chains, in order<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>People get lost in the Indoor Playground because they treat the room as one puzzle with one answer. It is three chains that only touch at the cubby lockers, and each chain ends in something you can hold.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Balls → pipe board → blue key.</b> Four coloured balls, four cups, four routes. Nothing else on the board matters until all four are loaded, and the board will not pay out until all four routes are clean.</li>
      <li><b>Blue key → sneakers → shoelace → magnet → rod.</b> The only reason the sneakers exist is to be destroyed, and the only reason the magnet exists is to be tied to the lace. The rod is the tool that turns the ball pit from scenery into a container.</li>
      <li><b>Rod → second key → backpack → bulb → paper → chest → EXIT.</b> This is the chain that hides the code, and it is the one that makes the level feel twice as long as it is, because it crosses into a second area that the playroom never advertises.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Looked at that way the level has exactly two puzzles — the pipe board and the gumball dials — and one number. Everything else is a dependency check dressed as furniture, which is why the same three questions come up under every video of this room: where are the balls, what is the second key for, and where is the code.</p>

    <div class="warn">
      <b>What we could not verify:</b> the three angles on the gumball dials. The boat's life rings carry thin red marks at three different clock positions and the dials repeat them, but our capture is too small to read the rings twice with certainty, so this page does not print the angles — read them off the boat yourself. We also could not double-confirm which two props give the last two balls: our recording clearly shows the ball being taken out of the play area and out of the gumball machine, and the counter reaching <b>4/4</b>, but the other two pickups happen too fast to pin to a single prop. And the light bulb fitting itself is a two-second beat in the footage; the room going from dark to lit is clear, the exact tap is not.
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The four balls.</b> The pipe board shows four empty cups and the room never says "four". The inventory counter is the only progress bar you get: 3/4 means keep tapping props, 4/4 means go back to the board.</li>
      <li><b>The boat.</b> The life rings on the toy boat are the clue for the gumball dials, and the boat is small, low and easy to walk past. If the dials make no sense, you have not looked at the rings.</li>
      <li><b>The sneakers.</b> They look like an exit item because they are shoes and the EXIT sign is two metres away. They are not: <b>Dismantle</b> them.</li>
      <li><b>The combine order.</b> Lace first, then magnet, then rod. Trying to put the bare magnet on the rod does nothing, and the game gives you no error message — it just does not combine.</li>
      <li><b>The ball pit.</b> The rod with the magnet goes into the pit, not into the pond, the drain or the hoppers. The pit is the only container in the room with something metal in it.</li>
      <li><b>The tunnel.</b> The plank on the right wall lifts on the pulley and the opening under it is a corridor. Players who never pull it finish the first half of the level and then hunt for a code that is in a different room entirely.</li>
      <li><b>The chest.</b> The four-wheel lock is on the chest in the dollshouse, not on the EXIT door. Entering 3764 on the door does nothing because the door has no lock of its own — it wants the key the chest gives you.</li>
    </ul>

